0.4.6

1. /#write/#write_csv/.  This is a temporary measure(I think?) until I can figure out to get #write to co-exist with IO#write.
2. /attr_read :lines/attr_accessor :lines/ for when assigning an out file the in file's values.  This seems pretty cludgy, but we'll go with it for now.


0.4.5

1. Finally on to the writing stuff.  Although I think the changes to each method might be of some use...
2. Swapped read_line and parse_line, for no other reason than consistency.
3. Added in a couple of compatibility parameters to #init which allow for setting mode and permissions on the call to this method in the File superclass.
4. Created methods write, write_line, and to_csv which are all to support dumping of data to a CSV file as per Goal#1.
5. Created a method #lines? to support nicer querying as to whether there is any data read from the CSV file.
6. Took the debugging and reformatted #each a little.
7. Added an alias #read? for #lines?.
8. Added in && ['r', 'r+'].include?(mode) to the line of #initialize which sets the column names, since if a file is not open for reading, then I shouldn't be trying to read from it!
9. Changed #to_csv, such that it now doesn't try to add files to the collector if that column isn't specified.  Now, by way of using columns and not @columns, whereas before I had this silly if include thing?...
10. Added format/quote to the #init interface---pushing the standard file paramters yet further up the chain.  I may reorder these, but as they have defaults...?
11. There's a conflict between my attempted use of File(< IO)#puts and CSVFile#write, since File#puts calls #write and an infinite loop, or till the stack is used up ensues.  It is working at the moment, but only if I don't call write, but use write_line instead (which was the case anyway) and if it is commented out!


0.4.4

1. Almost added the to_s I suspected was missing to make #each work with symbols, but realised why I didn't immediately need to do so.
2. Swapped out the def for rows for an alias on lines.


0.4.3

1. Removed #each_with_columns and (for backwards compatibility?) made it an alias for #each, whilst rolling in the bit of code with generates multiple return values.
2. The only thing lost by doing this is that it is not longer possible to specify which columns are to be collected and then to have a single line parameter returned to the block.  No great loss methinks.


